  sectioned design, and a written spec before any code. The [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter on [38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes one small consequence of
  that design rather than the change itself.

  So I'll set the three widget options aside — they were all answers to a one-file question. Let me build up the real
  picture, one question at a time.

  [1mFirst fork — what the ID identifies.[0m This determines the storage, the lifecycle, and the privacy posture, and it's the
  hardest thing to change later:

  - [1mAuthenticated user identity[0m — the ID [3mis[0m the account, issued by the server at login. Persists for the session,
    cleared on logout. Buys real attribution ("Alice did this"). Costs a server contract you don't have yet ([38;5;153mlogin()[39m is
    a stub), and it doesn't exist before someone logs in — so forms on a logged-out page have nothing.
  - [1mAnonymous device/visitor ID[0m — generated client-side on first visit, stored, survives reloads and logout. Buys
    coverage everywhere including logged-out forms, and needs no backend. Costs: it identifies a browser, not a person,
    and it's a tracking identifier with the privacy/consent implications that carries.
  - [1mBoth, linked[0m — a device ID always present, associated with the account ID once login succeeds. This is what
    analytics systems actually do. Costs the most structure: two IDs, a lifecycle for linking them, and rules for what
    happens on logout.

  My recommendation is [1mboth, linked[0m, specifically because you said other forms will need it. Forms on logged-out pages
  can only use a device ID, and the login form is exactly where the two meet — designing for one now and bolting on the
  other later means reworking every call site.
